# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
sensible	one
perceived_by_sense	two	perceive	P2	what our senses are aimed at in ordinary seeing and touching, such as houses and rivers	what is immediately present to the mind in sensing, the direct object of awareness
idea	one
can_exist_unperceived	one
# perceived_by_sense: this is the familiar charge against the passage. In P1 houses count as perceived because we see and touch them. In P2 only what is immediately before the mind counts as perceived. Read in the ordinary sense, P2 would be false; read in the immediate sense, P1 assumes the conclusion. The text does not mark the two senses itself, so this rests on reading "perceive" in S3 one way in the first question and another way in the second.
